# Tropes a workspace installs

A trope written in one book reaches another book as a package. Loom publishes its own tropes the way it publishes its language services, as the chapter "The store of installed services" describes. Every trope Loom publishes sits in one package, `@athrio/loom-tropes`. `loom add mermaid` installs that package into the workspace's store and records the trope in `.loom/config.yaml`:

```
tropes:
  mermaid: {}
```

The package gives each trope a folder named by its id, and the folder carries the trope's looms in a `looms` folder, the same looms the trope's own book holds. Loom reads only the tropes the configuration names, though the package holds them all. Loom reads them as it reads any loom, and gathers the kinds the trope's root declares. A book that installs the Mermaid trope may then write a `Mermaid` box in any of its looms. The trope's chapters stay outside the book's corpus all the same: they join no table of contents, no woven page, and no tangle of the book.

Reading a package waits on files, so Loom reads a workspace's installed tropes once, before the first judgement that needs them, and keeps what it read. Every later judgement looks the kinds up in memory, as it looks up the kinds the book's own tropes declare.

## Reading a package's looms

`TropePackages` is the service that reads installed tropes, and its `kindsFor` method gives the kinds that the workspace of a loom installs. `LoomConfig` resolves the loom's workspace and the tropes the workspace names. `kindsFor` keeps the kinds for that workspace once it has read them, under a key that `keyFor` writes from the workspace, each trope, and the version of it the store holds. Installing, removing, or updating a trope changes the key, so the next judgement reads the store again.

`installedKinds` reads the kinds of a set of tropes. `loomsIn` lists the looms of one trope, from the `looms` folder in the trope's folder of the package, ordered by name. `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` builds each loom into a module through `fromDisk`, a source that reads the file. `declaredKindsOf` then gathers the kinds the modules declare, over a book of the packages' own looms, so two installed tropes clash as two tropes of one book do.

A package the registry refuses adds no kinds. No loom of the workspace holds the package's text for an editor to mark, so Loom logs the reason as a warning instead.

=>

export interface TropePackagesApi {
  readonly kindsFor: (loom: Location) => Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>>
}

const fromDisk: Source = {
  read: (path) =>
    Effect.try({
      try: () => readFileSync(path, 'utf8'),
      catch: (cause) => new ReadError({ path, cause }),
    }),
  list: Option.none(),
}

const loomsIn = (folder: string): ReadonlyArray<Location> =>
  pipe(
    Option.liftThrowable(() => readdirSync(folder))(),
    Option.map((names) =>
      pipe(
        names,
        Array.filter((name) => name.endsWith('.loom')),
        Array.sort(Order.String),
        Array.map((name) => join(folder, name)),
      ),
    ),
    Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Location> => []),
  )

const keyFor = (workspace: Location, tropes: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  JSON.stringify([
    workspace,
    ...Array.map(tropes, (id) => [id, Option.getOrNull(installedTropeVersion(id, workspace))]),
  ])

export class TropePackages extends Context.Service<TropePackages>()('TropePackages', {
  make: Effect.gen(function* () {
    const config = yield* LoomConfig
    const builder = yield* LoomCorpusAstBuilder
    const kept = yield* Ref.make<ReadonlyMap<string, ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>>>(new Map())

    const installedKinds = (
      workspace: Location,
      tropes: ReadonlyArray<string>,
    ): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>> =>
      Effect.forEach(
        Array.flatMap(tropes, (id) => loomsIn(join(tropeDir(id, workspace), 'looms'))),
        (path) =>
          builder.build(fromDisk, path).pipe(Effect.map((module) => [path, module] as const)),
      ).pipe(
        Effect.map((pairs): ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule> => new Map(pairs)),
        Effect.map((modules) => declaredKindsOf(modules, bookOf(modules))),
        Effect.tap((kinds) =>
          Effect.forEach(
            kinds.refusals,
            (refusal) =>
              Effect.logWarning(
                `loom: the installed trope in ${refusal.module} is refused: ${refusal.reason}`,
              ),
            { discard: true },
          ),
        ),
        Effect.map((kinds) => kinds.box),
      )

    const kindsFor = (loom: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>> =>
      config.resolve(loom).pipe(
        Effect.flatMap(({ workspaceRoot, tropes }) =>
          Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(workspaceRoot), {
            onNone: () => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>>([]),
            onSome: (workspace) => {
              const ids = Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(tropes), () => [])
              const key = keyFor(workspace, ids)
              return Ref.get(kept).pipe(
                Effect.flatMap((known) =>
                  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(known.get(key)), {
                    onSome: (kinds) => Effect.succeed(kinds),
                    onNone: () =>
                      installedKinds(workspace, ids).pipe(
                        Effect.tap((kinds) =>
                          Ref.update(kept, (was) => new Map(was).set(key, kinds)),
                        ),
                      ),
                  }),
                ),
              )
            },
          }),
        ),
      )

    const packages: TropePackagesApi = { kindsFor }
    return packages
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make).pipe(
    Layer.provide(LoomCorpusAstBuilder.layer),
  )
}

## What the service draws on

From `effect` come the array, option, and order combinators, `Ref` for the kinds the service keeps, and the service and layer machinery. Node's `fs` lists a package's looms and reads each one, and `path` joins the folders. `LoomConfig` resolves a loom's workspace and the tropes it names. The store lends `tropeDir`, the folder of a trope's package, and `installedTropeVersion`, the version of it the store holds. `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` builds a loom from a `Source`, whose `ReadError` a failed read raises. The corpus model lends `bookOf` and the types of a module and its location, and the registry lends `declaredKindsOf` with the `DeclaredKind` it gathers.

=>

import { Array, Context, Effect, Layer, Option, Order, pipe, Ref } from 'effect'
import { readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { LoomConfig } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import { installedTropeVersion, tropeDir } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import { bookOf, type Location, type LoomModule } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { LoomCorpusAstBuilder, ReadError, type Source } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { type DeclaredKind, declaredKindsOf } from '#ast/DeclaredKinds'

